------------------------
test vs test.only in Playwright
------------------------

1. test

test() is used to define a normal test case.

Example:

import { test } from '@playwright/test';

test('Login Test', async ({ page }) => {
    // Test steps
});

test('Search Test', async ({ page }) => {
    // Test steps
});

When you run: npx playwright test

both tests will run.

Login Test
    ↓
Search Test

2. test.only

test.only() is used when you want to run only a specific test and skip the other tests in the test file/suite.

Example:

test.only('Login Test', async ({ page }) => {
    // Test steps
});

test('Search Test', async ({ page }) => {
    // Test steps
});

When you run: npx playwright test

Only: Login Test will run.

Search Test will be skipped because test.only() has been used.

------------------------
ASSERTIONS
------------------------

Assertions are used to verify whether the actual result matches the expected result.

In simple words:

Assertion = Check / Verify


Import:

import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';


test
    → Used to define a test case.

expect
    → Used to perform assertions/verification.


------------------------
TITLE PRINT
------------------------

To print the actual page title:

console.log(await page.title());


Example:

test('Title Print', async ({ page }) => {

    await page.goto('https://example.com');

    console.log(await page.title());

});


console.log()
    ↓
Prints the actual title
    ↓
Does NOT verify whether the title is correct.


------------------------
TITLE VERIFY / ASSERTION
------------------------

To verify the page title:

await expect(page).toHaveTitle('Example Domain');


Example:

test('Title Verification', async ({ page }) => {

    await page.goto('https://example.com');

    await expect(page).toHaveTitle('Example Domain');

});


expect()
    ↓
Verifies the expected result


If actual title = expected title
    ↓
Test PASSES


If actual title ≠ expected title
    ↓
Test FAILS


------------------------
EASY WAY TO REMEMBER
------------------------

console.log()
    ↓
PRINT
    ↓
"What is the actual value?"


expect()
    ↓
VERIFY / ASSERT
    ↓
"Is the actual value what I expected?"

------------------------
headless: false
------------------------

headless controls whether the browser is visible during test execution.

use: {
    headless: false,
}

Means:

Show the browser while the test is running.

Simple:

headless: true
    → Browser hidden

headless: false
    → Browser visible
	
Some commonly used ones are:

use: {
    browserName: 'chromium',
    headless: false,
    screenshot: 'on',
    video: 'on',
    trace: 'on',
    baseURL: 'https://example.com',
}

Simple meaning

| Setting       | Meaning                                     |
| ------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| `browserName` | Which browser to use                        |
| `headless`    | Show/hide browser                           |
| `screenshot`  | Take screenshots                            |
| `video`       | Record test execution                       |
| `trace`       | Capture detailed test execution information |
| `baseURL`     | Common URL for your tests                   |
